Bacteriophage life cycles — licensing
Copyright (c) 2026 Sean Nair

This project is released under two licences, because the same files hold two
different kinds of work: a piece of software, and the teaching material it
draws.


SOFTWARE — MIT License          (full text in LICENSE-MIT)

    The HTML, CSS and JavaScript considered as source code: the rendering
    engine, the timeline and easing machinery, the camera, the procedural
    Web Audio soundtrack, the transport controls and the page layout.

    Fork it, adapt it, build your own animations on it, commercially or
    otherwise. Keep the copyright notice. No attribution beyond that notice
    is required.


CONTENT — Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)
                                (details in LICENSE-CC-BY-4.0)

    The expressive material: the written captions, annotation labels, phase
    descriptions and end-card text — including where that text sits inside
    the source files; the visual design of the animations; and any still
    image, screen recording, frame grab or other capture of them, including
    the screenshots in docs/ and the prose of this README.

    Share it and adapt it, for any purpose including commercially, but give
    appropriate credit, link to the licence, and say if you changed it.


THE SHORT VERSION

    Reusing the machinery needs no credit. Reusing the words or the pictures
    does. If you are unsure which licence covers a particular use, crediting
    the author satisfies both.


ATTRIBUTION

    A suggested form, for a slide, a handout or a caption:

        "Bacteriophage life cycles" by Sean Nair, CC BY 4.0
        https://github.com/seannair/Bacteriophage-life-cycle-animation

    If you modified the animation, please say so, so that any errors you
    introduce are not attributed to the original.


A NOTE ON THE SPLIT

    The boundary is between the program and what the program expresses. Code
    that draws a bacterium is software and is MIT. The picture of the
    bacterium it produces, and the sentence explaining what the bacterium is
    doing, are content and are CC BY 4.0. In practice: a developer forking
    the engine is working under MIT; a teacher putting a frame or a caption
    into a lecture is working under CC BY 4.0.
